WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.300
Why we need HR-driven ICAM
at all — and why now.

2
00:00:03.300 --> 00:00:06.982
Zero Trust is federal
strategy — OMB M-22-09,

3
00:00:06.982 --> 00:00:15.163
with NIST SP 800-207 as the architecture
and CISA's maturity model as the yardstick.

4
00:00:15.163 --> 00:00:20.136
SASE is one way these controls can be
delivered near users and cloud resources,

5
00:00:20.136 --> 00:00:25.637
which TIC 3.0 opens up for agencies,
instead of only at our network perimeter.

6
00:00:25.637 --> 00:00:30.309
Reliable ICAM supplies critical
identity signals to those decisions:

7
00:00:30.309 --> 00:00:34.521
the user presents a token from a
PIV card or phishing-resistant MFA,

8
00:00:34.521 --> 00:00:38.120
and Entra ID decides access
through Conditional Access

9
00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:41.337
and dynamic groups that
read the user's attributes.

10
00:00:41.337 --> 00:00:45.612
In the perimeter era, a wrong department
meant a wrong phone-book entry.

11
00:00:45.612 --> 00:00:49.058
Now it means the wrong groups,
and so the wrong access —

12
00:00:49.058 --> 00:00:51.462
and a stale status is worse than ever.

13
00:00:51.462 --> 00:00:54.592
In the perimeter era an
orphaned account was already a

14
00:00:54.592 --> 00:00:57.862
standing security violation
that could enable a breach.

15
00:00:57.862 --> 00:01:00.662
Now it's a valid token
for someone who has left,

16
00:01:00.662 --> 00:01:04.825
usable from anywhere, because there
is no perimeter left to stop it.

17
00:01:04.825 --> 00:01:07.749
When access decisions
depend on attributes,

18
00:01:07.749 --> 00:01:12.000
the accuracy of employee data
drives the accuracy of access.
